  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Road Paint",
    "overview": "Voltway is a highway at night, and the trip is the paint on it. Surfaces are asphalt charcoal; the one strong colour is centre-line yellow, kept for what the visitor should follow: the planned route, the primary action, the current page. Road-marking white draws stop bars and Level 2 chargers, and construction orange flags trouble (a charging gap, an unavailable charger). The light theme is the same road in daylight: pale concrete surfaces with the same roles, and yellow text turning dark amber where yellow would not read.\n\nThe interface is an Operate tool, used before a trip on a phone or laptop. It is calm and dense rather than decorative: the map fills the screen, and the planner card, navigation pill and map controls float over it on small rounded surfaces. Precision shows in tabular figures, a source badge on every vehicle figure, dashed lines for estimates, and a missing charger drawn as plainly as a present one.\n\nIt deliberately refuses the category default of a dark navy map app with a green accent, and it has no blue, teal or green interface colours. The exceptions are the comparison series and the blue \"you are here\" dot.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Asphalt charcoal and concrete surfaces; one loud colour, centre-line yellow.",
      "Filled yellow buttons always carry dark ink labels.",
      "Dashed means \"not certain\": estimated curves, charging gaps.",
      "Floating pill and card surfaces over a full-screen map.",
      "Archivo throughout, with tabular figures for every number."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Paint Rule",
        "body": "Centre-line yellow marks what to follow: the trip, the primary action, the current place. If two yellow things compete on a screen, one of them is wrong. While a trip shows, charger marks turn grey so the trip is the only yellow on the map (B54).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ink-On-Paint Rule",
        "body": "Anything filled yellow carries Paint Ink (#1A1B1E) text or glyphs, never white.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Amber-In-Daylight Rule",
        "body": "Yellow is never used for text on concrete; the light theme uses Amber Text (#6F5300) for links and focus.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One-Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Orange means a problem, violet means an estimate, red means Tesla. None of them is decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Every number a visitor compares (times, kWh, kW, km) uses tabular figures.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Muted-Suffix Rule",
        "body": "A variant name's code suffix (\"-B (pre-6/11/25)\") is shown as a separate, muted span after the plain name (4.16), never in the same weight.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Float-Only Rule",
        "body": "Shadows mean \"floating over the map\". Cards on content pages stay flat.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep Centre-Line Yellow (#FFD400) for the trip, the primary action and the current place, and grey out chargers while a trip shows.",
      "Do put Paint Ink (#1A1B1E) on every yellow fill.",
      "Do switch text accents to Amber Text (#6F5300) in the light theme.",
      "Do dash anything estimated or uncertain, and label it \"Estimated\".",
      "Do show a source badge on every vehicle figure.",
      "Do use tabular figures for numbers people compare.",
      "Do add every new colour pair to `tests/contrast.test.ts` (4.5:1 text, 3:1 graphics and controls)."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use blue, teal or green for interface chrome; the comparison series and the location dot are the only exceptions.",
      "Don't put yellow text on concrete or white text on yellow.",
      "Don't use orange, violet or red for anything but warnings, estimates and Tesla pins.",
      "Don't add shadows to flat content cards; shadows mean floating over the map.",
      "Don't show speed or plug tags on groups of 20 or more chargers."
    ]
  }
}
